   10
  ],
  "printed": "Romans v. 10",
  "page": 392,
  "line": 15,
  "situation": "And three lines later the Latin must be refused. The Greek has the Father give the Son for a ransom *ὑπ' ἐχθρῶν*, at the hands of enemies; the Latin opposite reads *pro hostibus*, for enemies — the familiar theology of Romans v. 10. *ὑπέρ* does not elide to *ὑπ'*, so the Greek cannot be read as the Latin reads it. The English follows the Greek and leaves the crux standing, immediately after the same column twice rescued it.",
  "source": "identified",
  "band": "R"
 },
